This package repackages dsh-recall by the dsh-recall authors, used under the
MIT License. The original project lives at:

    https://github.com/omdsh-dev/dsh-recall

Upstream publishes this plugin prebuilt, with no sources, so vendor/ holds its
published lib/ output verbatim and scripts/build.mjs adapts it. Every edit is
an asserted exact-string rewrite, so a changed upstream line fails the build
rather than silently shipping something unintended.

Renamed to @my-dsh/rewind, and the route with it: POST /recall is now
POST /rewind. The client bundle's __ModuleLoader__ id follows the package
name, because the web boot manifest looks a bundle up by that name.

Two identities are deliberately NOT renamed. `session/recall` is the harness's
own durable event type, and the `recall` node kind is matched against it; both
belong to the runtime rather than to this package. Renaming either would break
the fold that renders the "recalled message" notice.

English only. Upstream registers a Simplified Chinese dictionary alongside
English; the English dictionary is now registered under both locale ids, so
selecting Chinese leaves the harness in Chinese and this plugin in English
rather than rendering raw message keys from an unregistered namespace.

The client is repackaged rather than rewritten, unlike the other ports here.
The rewind button sits on every user message, which means shadowing the
framework's keyed user-bubble renderer and reproducing the bubble — content
blocks, image gallery, lightbox and all. Upstream already does that faithfully;
redoing it would be risk without benefit.

The `/rewind` command and its message picker are not upstream at all. They live
in the companion @my-dsh/rewind-picker package, which drives this plugin's
route.

What this does NOT do: revert files. The log keeps every event and nothing on
disk is touched, so code the agent wrote during a rewound turn stays exactly as
it is. This is upstream's design, and it is the one real difference from the
rewind feature in Claude Code, which also restores the working tree.

